## This session

**`defects.sh`** — top level, read-only, alongside `run.sh`/`showstat.sh`. Reads `tests/criteria/OPEN_DEFECTS.json`; its numbers reproduce the board gate exactly (`open=25 of=64`, same id set). Search widens id-in-scope → id-anywhere → full-text and says which level answered, so an id lookup reaches a defect closed last week without `-a`; no match exits 1 instead of printing nothing. Also added a "THE LEDGER" paragraph to RULE 6 in CLAUDE.md — the rule stated the policy but never named the file, so a session learned the path only from the state hook or by grepping.

**The AG escalation.** The sess106 root (direct EX handoff grants without minting `ex_grant_epoch`) is wider than recorded: the direct-handoff block has **no resource-type gate**, and `xfs/xfs_mxfs_dlm.c:35083` reads the AG token as a separate post-acquire I/O into `pag->pag_mxfs_grant_epoch`, which `xfs_buf_item_format_segment` stamps lock-free into every buffer-log record of the tenure. On any AG EX acquired by handoff, those records carry **another node's epoch** — not a missing token, a wrong one, which reads as legitimate authority to a replayer.

**RULE-5 ruling** (saved as `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess107-GPT-ruling-direct-handoff-mint-10-blockers`): the releaser **may** mint the grantee's epoch — the CAS is the linearization point, the issuing node is irrelevant, and adoption is recognition of a durable grant rather than creation of authority, so the handoff keeps its performance reason for existing. But 10 release blockers, not two lines. The sharpest: reconcile must be bound to the specific acquire attempt and compare epoch+mode, not just the node bit — *"if the existing abort reconcile can remove a live node's holder based only on an abandoned waiter observation, direct handoff is not safe, with or without the epoch fix."* And the mixed-version gate can't be a local module param, since an old node can be the releaser.

**One new critical defect filed** — `D-EX-GRANT-EPOCH-NOT-UNIQUE-TENURE-ID`. The token documented as uniquely naming a tenure is assigned from the uint32 slot `generation`, so it's a zero-extended cyclic value: it repeats after 2^32 CASes, and at the wrap mints 0 — the value the protocol defines as invalid, making a live legitimate tenure report no authority. `generation` advances on every CAS including waiter registration, so hot resources reach that far sooner than a tenure count implies. Shipped since sess48.

I added a caveat GPT didn't have: its `+1` sequence design regresses unless `caw_tombstone_slot` also preserves `ex_grant_epoch` (it preserves `generation` but zeroes the epoch today), or the namespace restarts at 1 on every tombstone+reclaim.

No code written yet — the tree is at 0.11.437 with no half-finished edits. Next is implementing blockers 1–2 (mint in the handoff CAS, helper takes the grantee explicitly) together with the epoch-source change, since they share one mint point.

